external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/abdulrafaymohammed/Documents/Privat/Hackathons/EHL Final/Final-Hack-EHL","sessionId":"944dad4c-792b-4da5-b8a1-9392d6587dfe","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"main","slug":"misty-brewing-feather"}
{"parentUuid":"d0558b3b-4db4-46f9-9434-12564715324c","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"dc0fdfa8-4ff9-4069-9dde-a9ed6a702909","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015YaK3c14J3fui77Zp6oTer","type":"tool_result","content":"    1898 Audios/final final slide audio.txt\n(Transcribed by TurboScribe. Go Unlimited to remove this message.)\n\nOkay, so the pitch is going to be like this. I will start with the demo where I'll be showing, where I'll be setting up some upfront requirements, I'll be giving it some input data, then I'll get some requirements proposed, I'll check on the reasoning, then I'll arrange something and give one reason or as to why I arranged it, save it, and then I have it approved, and then I'll say, this is what the scope of the challenge was, but this is also what everybody else did. So, after we built this, we talked to product managers.\n\nWe're going to say, we talked to the product managers after we built this first prototype, and specifically one of them, an ex-product manager at Vodafone, a common issue among the product managers was that they often don't have the context on the past decisions and why they were made, especially when onboarding a new product manager into a project. And then we'll display the quote of the ex-product manager at Vodafone, and then we'll go through the project diary to onboard somebody new, and then on the second run, we will again fill out some upfront requirements, then get to see the requirements, and then in the reasoning, there should also be a section on memory or past decisions, so they have a weightage into the reasoning and the ranking, and this would be our competitive edge. And then some creative ending statements, basically, and that would, in the best case, tie back to how we're not replacing product managers with actually helping them or focussing on the competitive edge we have, which is memory, past decisions, and onboarding of new product managers and the handover part, basically, which, after a bit of research, we realised happens quite often, especially because some projects just take years to do.\n\n(Transcribed by TurboScribe. Go Unlimited to remove this message.)\nShell cwd was reset to /Users/abdulrafaymohammed/Documents/Privat/Hackathons/EHL Final/Final-Hack-EHL","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"d1bef3e7-f3f6-4789-88c3-b662c7e898c9","timestamp":"2026-10-11T08:17:33.026Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"    1898 Audios/final final slide audio.txt\n(Transcribed by TurboScribe. Go Unlimited to remove this message.)\n\nOkay, so the pitch is going to be like this. I will start with the demo where I'll be showing, where I'll be setting up some upfront requirements, I'll be giving it some input data, then I'll get some requirements proposed, I'll check on the reasoning, then I'll arrange something and give one reason or as to why I arranged it, save it, and then I have it approved, and then I'll say, this is what the scope of the challenge was, but this is also what everybody else did. So, after we built this, we talked to product managers.\n\nWe're going to say, we talked to the product managers after we built this first prototype, and specifically one of them, an ex-product manager at Vodafone, a common issue among the product managers was that they often don't have the context on the past decisions and why they were made, especially when onboarding a new product manager into a project. And then we'll display the quote of the ex-product manager at Vodafone, and then we'll go through the project diary to onboard somebody new, and then on the second run, we will again fill out some upfront requirements, then get to see the requirements, and then in the reasoning, there should also be a section on memory or past decisions, so they have a weightage into the reasoning and the ranking, and this would be our competitive edge. And then some creative ending statements, basically, and that would, in the best case, tie back to how we're not replacin