ber has a source, competitors included: **nine signals** from Mercedes and Audi, each linked to the original. | **→** |\n| 9 | 1:03–1:07 | Back to the reasoning | But the AI only proposes. **The PM decides.** | **Move up** |\n| 10 | 1:07–1:12 | Seat comfort moves to #1 | Seats matter more than audio settings, so seat comfort goes to **number one**. | **Approve** |\n| 11 | 1:12–1:24 | Dictation: the reason appears word by word | And I say why, out loud. **One sentence**, transcribed and saved with the decision. *(let the words finish)* | **Approve requirement** |\n| 12 | 1:24–1:29 | The card shows Approved | Approved, **with its reason**. | **→** |\n\n## The turn\n\n| # | Time | On screen | Say | Click |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 13 | 1:29–1:39 | Slide: \"That was the brief. And what every team built.\" | That was the brief: evidence in, ranked requirements with reasoning, the PM decides. Honestly, **that is what every team built.** So we went back to product managers. | **→** |\n| 14 | 1:39–1:54 | Slide: the quote appears word by word | A former product manager at **Vodafone** told us this. *(pause, let the audience read)* Projects run for years. **PMs change. And the \"why\" gets lost.** | **→** |\n| 15 | 1:54–1:58 | Slide: \"Six months later. A handover.\" | So: **six months later**, a new PM takes over. | **→** |\n\n## Demo 2: the memory\n\n| # | Time | On screen | Say | Click |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 16 | 1:58–2:03 | Project Diary; April activity on the calendar | Day one, the new PM opens the **Project Diary**: every decision on this car, day by day. | **Storyline** |\n| 17 | 2:03–2:07 | Storyline menu | We pick **Onboarding**… | **Onboarding** |\n| 18 | 2:07–2:10 | Date range filled in | …from the first decision until today. | **Generate storyline** |\n| 19 | 2:10–2:18 | The written storyline, zoomed | And the project tells its own story: **what was decided, when, and why**, instead of chasing colleagues for weeks. | **→** |\n| 20 | 2:18–2:26 | The new PM types 24 months, 8 people | The new PM sets **their own constraints**: 24 months, 8 people. | **→** |\n| 21 | 2:26–2:29 | \"Generate the requirements\" | Generate again. **Nothing uploaded.** | **Generate requirements** |\n| 22 | 2:29–2:40 | Seat comfort leads, tagged **Past decision** | Seat comfort leads, and it carries a tag: **past decision**. Let us look inside. | **Evidence & reasoning** |\n| 23 | 2:40–2:46 | Decision Memory block, zoomed | There it is: **approved on 14 April by the previous PM**, with the reason in their own words. | **→** |\n| 24 | 2:46–2:51 | Score bar, zoomed on \"Past decisions\" | And it is not just a note. **Past decisions count in the ranking**, right next to customers and competitors. | **→** |\n\n## Close\n\n| # | Time | On screen | Say | Click |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 25 | 2:51–3:00 | Close: \"Vehicle projects run for years.\" | Vehicle projects run for years. People change. **Decisions shouldn't get lost.** We don't replace product managers. **We give them the memory of everyone who came before.** Thank you. | – |\n\n---\n\n### If a judge asks during Q&A\n\n- **\"Is the data real?\"** The 4,365 findings, 88 customers and 9 competitor signals come from BMW's G60 files and the competitor workbook. The uploaded customer comments are labelled demo data, and the app always shows them as \"Your data\".\n- **\"Did you really speak?\"** The app supports live dictation in the browser; in this recording the speech input is simulated, because it was recorded without a microphone.\n- **\"Why 5 points but 9 signals?\"** Points are the score; signals are the sources behind it. More signals add points up to a cap, so no single source type can dominate.\n- **\"What does a past decision do to the score?\"** An approved decision adds 5 points to that requirement for the next PM; a rejected one adds nothing and is flagged with \"check whether that reason still holds\".\n"}},"serverClassifierRequest":"685d0acb-0b64-4506-9bf6-3f25e294ed30","apiBlockI